Method (standing): one focused change per variant; the control
is byte-identical code; rosters alternate champion/challenger
seats with a random league seat as spoiler; two 20-episode legs
swap the seat pairs so seat order cannot explain a result.ROUND 1 — Entry (2026-11-23, EE ~362)

Built emise-cards-and-casters:v1 from the bundled Archer starter
(sha 566bfe28) with one change: in the swing routine, a hero
swing with myPower >= enemyLife scores 100 (always take lethal);
the starter's flat 4 + 2*power could trade into a minion instead
of ending the game.

First hosted test (xreq_4fc0730b, 4 episodes, our policy in
seats 0-2, league-filled veterans in 3-4): our seats won 2 of 4
episodes (winners elsewhere: Rohit orch v2, stilt v30).

A/B vs the pristine starter (control uploaded byte-identical as
emise-cac-starter:v1, version 2a4f71a9): 8 episodes, seat wins
v1 3, starter 2, random 3 — inside noise.

Training run (xreq_efcac072, 40 episodes, $0.11): seat wins
v1 20 of 80 appearances (25%), starter 8 of 80 (10%), random
veteran seat 12 of 40 (30%: A-aron v142 4, salvo v1 3,
macromackie v64 3, Rohit orch v2 2). Head-to-head v1 took 20 of
28 non-random episodes (p ~ 0.02 under a coin-flip null): the
lethal change is a genuine improvement over the starter. The
veteran field still leads per-seat; headroom remains.ROUND 2 — Scout study; v2 falsified (2026-11-23, EE ~367)

Study method: hosted logs carry no decisions, so a scout variant
(v1 logic + PRINT of the public event stream — attacks, damage,
deaths, defeats, with targets) ran 12 episodes in seats 0-3
(xreq_5028a675), ~24k trace lines. Findings: the veteran seat
won only 2 of 12 (both stilt v30, Warrior); its big win was a
pure face-race (50 hero damage, 100% hero attacks), but across
all 12 games the winner held top hero damage only ~1/3 of the
time, and two veterans dealt the most hero damage and lost.
Face-racing: one veteran's style, not the winning formula.

Code finding: the game docs state enemyPlayer is the NEXT
LIVING player. v1's lethal check reads enemyLife only, so it
recognizes eliminations against just one of four enemy heroes.

v2 (emise-cards-and-casters:v2, version 123b3b3f): one line —
lethal judged against the target hero's own life, playerLife(o).
A/B vs v1 (xreq_869cd6ae + xreq_7c11dd22, 40 episodes,
seat-swapped legs, $0.086): v1 23 seat wins, v2 13, random 4;
both legs agreed (10-6, 13-7). VERDICT: falsified. A flat 100
for any elimination spends big swings finishing weak heroes
instead of killing the minions actually threatening us; the
next-player focus encodes the ring's real geometry. v1 keeps
the championship. Negative result recorded deliberately: it is
the evidence the v3 bracket is built on.CLAIMS REGISTRY — MOAT/TLUF discipline (added EE 368, Will's direction)

TLUF: Truth, Lies, Unknown, Fiction — the Oracle's four bins.
Every claim this lineage carries holds exactly one status,
assigned by a basis that constrains it (MOAT's Minimum Viable
Oracle discipline): STIPULATION or VERIFIED -> True; REFUTED ->
False; ATTRIBUTED, OPEN, SYNTHESIS, ESTIMATE -> Unknown;
CONSTRUCTION -> Fiction. Statuses change only on receipts,
along legal edges, and every transition is recorded here.
False claims are kept visible, never deleted. No pruning: the
minimum is structure, never content. Counts at this writing:
True 14, False 7, Unknown 15, Fiction 2 (EE 424: U23/U24 entered — pricing saturation at the champion point; EE 419: U21/U22 entered on Round 22 screen receipts; EE 417: U20 entered on screen receipts; EE 388: the failure shelf distilled; U19 entered at design time; EE 386: U17 refuted — the package does not travel to Mage; EE 384: T15 graduated, U17/U18 entered, U5b measured; EE 383: U16 entered at design time; EE 375 MVO pass — the zero-uncertainty programme's first harvest: four claims closed, four new ones discovered at the frontier).

