     Screen addendum (EE 419, Round 22): the margin
     survives a strong field — with v7 in the fifth seat,
     v11 beat v6 2,356-1,245 (65.4% of decisive), against
     64.4% under reference fields. Not a soft-field
     artifact on this surface.
     League succession (EE 430): the pair-format crown
     does not enter the arena as-is — in a lone league
     seat the partner arithmetic would crown a stranger
     (partial immunity for one opponent, helpful effects
     allowed on their minions). The league form, v16,
     keeps the protocol's focus-fire with partner rules
     inert (one hunk: partner = -1). Lone-seat screen
     (10,000 matches, rotating seats, field of v6 and
     the three references): v16 took 52.1% of wins, v6
     33.2%. Submitted as champion on Will's word
     (sub_8d445f1, pending placement at inscription).
  U21 "The reach deck (v7's Bolt-splash width build)
     improves the champion-plus-protocol composition
     (v12 over v11)." OPEN — entered EE 419 with its
     screen receipts: v11 beat v12 8,020-5,233 across
     all three field flavors (v12 took 39.5% of
     decisive), while v12 still beat v6 (55.5%) and v7
     (61.1%). The screen reads the composition as an
     interaction, not a sum: the protocol has a native
     deck (width — full Banner/Footsoldier/Commander),
     and the reach deck's trims starve it. Hosted
     untested; the screen is decisive enough that no
     hosted series is proposed.
  U22 "Swapping Burnout into the Mage stock deck
     (Plan/Control trimmed to 2) improves it (v13)."
     OPEN — entered EE 419 with its screen receipts:
     v13 won 36 of 1,198 decisive against mage-stock
     (3.0%), and ~3% against v10 and v8 alike; the
     field took ~70%. Mechanism read at the source
     (baseset.nim): Burnout is a 0-cost Mage TRINKET
     whose rule deals 2 damage to its owner and tosses
     a card at each of the owner's turn-ends — the
     punishment token Overwork leaves when destroyed,
     deckable in the base set but excluded from the
     precon because playing it is self-harm by
     installments. Design error recorded: the swap was
     designed from the card's absence, without reading
     its rules text. Lesson: read the card.
  U23 "Raising the protocol's focus-fire swing bonus
     (3 to 6) improves v11 (v14)." OPEN — entered EE 424
     with its screen receipts: v14 took 49.6% of decisive
     against v11 over 13,331 (parity), 63.0% against v6 —
     v11's own marks. The probe sits inside the champion's
     equivalence class: at +3 the focus target was already
     the argmax nearly everywhere the bonus could bind.
  U24 "Cutting the chip-swing value (6 to 2) improves
     v11 (v15)." OPEN — entered EE 424 with its screen
     receipts: 49.7% against v11, 63.0% against v6, and
     tallies near-identical to v14's against every shared
     opponent (one pair byte-identical) — a second member
     of the same equivalence class. Together U23/U24 are
     the saturation measurement: at the champion point,
     the pricing surface is exhausted; what remains is
     structural (the score function, the protocol's
     shape) or hosted.
  U15 "The Archer and Splash stocks differ (class edge)."
     OPEN, no edge detected — xreq_154e2c7e + xreq_a259329c:
